---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/** The questions that start every trip — and the reason this site exists. */
const questions = [
  'Which cities should you stay in?',
  'How many days do you really need?',
  'Is that attraction worth spending half a day on?',
  'How much will the trip actually cost?',
  'Should you take the train, rent a car or fly?',
  'And how do you fit everything in without turning your holiday into a race?',
];

/** What friends and family actually ask — the site in five lines. */
const asks = [
  'How many days should we spend there?',
  'Where should we stay?',
  'Can we do these two places in the same day?',
  'What will the trip cost?',
  'Can you make an itinerary for us?',
];

const offers = [
  { icon: '🌍', title: 'Destination guides', body: 'Detailed itineraries designed around realistic travel.' },
  { icon: '💰', title: 'Realistic budgets', body: 'So you have an idea of what your trip could actually cost.' },
  { icon: '🛂', title: 'Visa and entry information', body: 'With links to official sources wherever possible.' },
  { icon: '🏨', title: 'Hotels and activities', body: 'Recommended options that you can book directly.' },
  { icon: '🗺️', title: 'Practical travel tips', body: 'The little details that make a big difference once you are actually there.' },
];
---
<Base
  title="About — The Itinerary Wala"
  description="Hi, I'm Nitesh. I build practical, day-by-day travel itineraries you can actually use — realistic budgets, honest visa notes, and no pretending I've been everywhere."
  image="/images/nitesh-himalaya.jpeg"
  imageAlt="Nitesh Parasher on a Himalayan pass"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>About</p>
      <h1 data-reveal>Travel is exciting. Planning it shouldn&rsquo;t be stressful.</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="author" data-reveal>
      <figure class="author__shot">
        <img
          src="/images/nitesh-himalaya.jpeg"
          alt="Nitesh Parasher holding an Indian flag on a Himalayan pass, snow peaks behind"
          width="675"
          height="900"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>On the pass, Indian Himalaya</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="author__body prose">
        <p class="author__hello">
          Hi, I&rsquo;m <strong>Nitesh</strong> — the person behind
          The Itinerary Wala.
        </p>
        <p>
          I&rsquo;ve always enjoyed travelling. But what I enjoy almost as much
          is figuring out how to make a trip actually work.
        </p>
        <ul class="asks">
          {questions.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>That&rsquo;s where The Itinerary Wala started.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>From trekking trails to international trips</h2>
      <p>
        My love for travel started in India, especially in the mountains.
        I&rsquo;ve travelled across almost every part of the country, and spent
        years exploring places through trekking, road trips and independent
        travel.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over time that curiosity took me beyond India — to places including
        Japan, Russia, Georgia, Armenia and the UAE. Every trip taught me the
        same thing:
      </p>
      <p class="pull">
        A great holiday isn&rsquo;t about seeing the maximum number of places.
        It&rsquo;s about getting the right things in the right order.
      </p>
      <p>
        That sounds simple. But building a genuinely useful itinerary takes a
        surprising amount of work.
      </p>

      <h2>So I started building the itineraries I wanted myself</h2>
      <p>
        A lot of travel content tells you where to go. I wanted something that
        also answered: <em>how do I actually do this trip?</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        So The Itinerary Wala focuses on practical, day-by-day travel plans —
        not just a list of attractions. You&rsquo;ll find realistic day-by-day
        schedules, recommended places to stay, travel times between
        destinations, suggested tours and activities, approximate trip budgets,
        transport options, visa and entry information, and the practical tips
        that save time and money.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is simple: you should be able to take an itinerary, adjust it
        to your style, and actually use it to plan your trip.
      </p>

      <h2>I don&rsquo;t pretend I&rsquo;ve been everywhere</h2>
      <p>
        This one matters to me. Some of the destinations here are places
        I&rsquo;ve personally travelled to. Others I&rsquo;ve researched
        extensively but haven&rsquo;t visited yet. I don&rsquo;t want to pretend
        otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        For destinations I&rsquo;ve travelled myself, I bring my own experience
        into the itinerary. For the rest, I research them the way I&rsquo;d
        research my own holiday — official sources, current information,
        transport schedules, prices and trusted travel resources.
      </p>
      <p>
        And whenever something is uncertain or likely to change, I&rsquo;ll tell
        you. I&rsquo;d rather say <em>&ldquo;check this before you
        travel&rdquo;</em> than confidently give you the wrong answer. Entry
        rules especially depend on your passport and where you live, and they
        change with very little notice — every plan says when it was last
        checked, but please confirm with the embassy or your airline before you
        book anything expensive.
      </p>

      <h2>Why &ldquo;The Itinerary Wala&rdquo;?</h2>
      <p>
        Because that&rsquo;s really what I am. I&rsquo;m the person friends and
        family come to when they&rsquo;re planning a trip.
      </p>
      <ul class="asks asks--quoted">
        {asks.map((a) => <li>{a}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>
        After doing it enough times I thought: why not build it for everyone?
        And that&rsquo;s how The Itinerary Wala came to life.
      </p>

      <h2>What I believe about travel</h2>
      <p>
        I don&rsquo;t think every trip needs to be luxurious. I don&rsquo;t
        think you need to visit every attraction. And I definitely don&rsquo;t
        think a holiday should feel like you&rsquo;re completing a checklist.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sometimes the best part of a trip is a slow morning, a great local
        restaurant, a scenic train ride, or simply having enough time to enjoy
        where you are.
      </p>
      <p class="pull">
        The itinerary should support the trip — not become the trip.
      </p>

      <h2>What you&rsquo;ll find here</h2>
      <p>
        The site grows destination by destination. Every new itinerary takes
        time, because I don&rsquo;t want to publish pages just to increase the
        number of countries on the website. I&rsquo;d rather have fewer useful
        itineraries than hundreds of generic ones.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="offers" data-reveal>
      {offers.map((item, i) => (
        <li class="offers__item" style={`--stagger:${i}`}>
          <span class="offers__icon" aria-hidden="true">{item.icon}</span>
          <div>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>How it pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        Some of the booking links are affiliate links. If you book through one,
        this site earns a small commission and you pay exactly the same price.
        There&rsquo;s no paid placement, and a hotel can&rsquo;t buy its way
        onto a plan.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="cta-card" data-reveal>
      <div>
        <h2>Planning your next trip?</h2>
        <p>
          Start with an itinerary. Pick a destination, see how many days you
          need, check the budget, and adjust the plan to make it yours. If the
          destination you want isn&rsquo;t here yet, ask me — it might be the
          next one I build.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn btn--solid" href="/itineraries/">Browse itineraries</a>
    </aside>

    <p class="sign-off" data-reveal>
      Travel less like a tourist. Plan more like a local.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
